{"uid":"src/css_length.c::cl_number","name":"cl_number","qualname":"cl_number","kind":"function","file":"src/css_length.c","line":292,"end_line":301,"language":"c","signature":"int cl_number(const char *s, double *out, const char **endp)","params":[{"name":"s","annotation":"char *","default":"","kind":"positional"},{"name":"out","annotation":"double *","default":"","kind":"positional"},{"name":"endp","annotation":"char **","default":"","kind":"positional"}],"returns":"int","decorators":[],"bases":[],"modifiers":[],"visibility":"public","doc":{"summary":"Parses a CSS <number> at the start of `s` (CSS Syntax Level 3, 4.3.12):","description":"[+-]?  ( digit+ ('.' digit*)?  |  '.' digit+ )  ( [eE] [+-]? digit+ )?\n\nReturns 1 on success, writing *out and pointing *endp at the first\nunconsumed character; 0 if there is no number there.\n\nExported because it is the same grammar for a length, a colour channel, an\nopacity and a transform argument, and having one implementation is what\nprevents them from disagreeing. The previous private parser required a digit\nBEFORE the decimal point, so the extremely common `.5` spelling -- as in\n`rgba(0,0,0,.5)`, `opacity:.75` or `padding:0 28px 0 .75em` -- was rejected\nand the whole declaration dropped.\n\nLocale-independent by construction: it never calls strtod, so a locale whose\ndecimal separator is ',' cannot truncate \"1.5px\" to 1px.","params":[],"returns":null,"yields":null,"raises":[],"attributes":[],"examples":[],"notes":[["Inherited","Documentation from `cl_number` (include/css_length.h:230)."]],"see_also":[],"deprecated":"","since":"","style":"plain"},"parent":"src/css_length.c","members":[],"inferred_raises":[],"returns_value":null,"value":"","base_uids":[],"subclasses":[],"calls":[],"called_by":[]}
